      "answer": "# How Photosynthesis Works: A Step-by-Step Guide\n\nPhotosynthesis is the process plants use to convert light energy into chemical energy (glucose). Here's how it works, broken down into its two main stages.\n\n## The Big Picture\n\n**Overall equation:**\n6CO\u2082 + 6H\u2082O + light energy \u2192 C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086 + 6O\u2082\n\nIn words: carbon dioxide + water + light \u2192 glucose + oxygen\n\nThis happens inside **chloroplasts**, organelles found mainly in leaf cells. Chloroplasts contain stacks of disc-like structures called **thylakoids** (where the light reactions happen) surrounded by a fluid called the **stroma** (where the Calvin cycle happens). The green pigment **chlorophyll** inside the thylakoids is what absorbs light.\n\n---\n\n## Stage 1: The Light Reactions (in the thylakoid membrane)\n\n**Goal:** Capture light energy and convert it into ATP and NADPH (energy-carrying molecules).\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Light absorption.** Chlorophyll absorbs photons (light particles). This excites electrons in the pigment to a higher energy level.\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Electron transport chain.** The excited electrons are passed down a series of proteins",
